# DTF tier manifest: repo-delete-reclaims-storage
#   artifact-keeper#4833: deleting a repository on filesystem storage leaves
#   its copy of any content another repository also holds on disk, and storage
#   GC never reclaims it.
#   Profile-set: storage.filesystem (each repository roots its own tree at
#   /data/storage/<key>, inspected with `docker exec find`).
#
# THE BUG
#   Generic artifacts are stored under a content-addressed key
#   (`ab/cd/<sha256>`), so two repositories holding the same bytes name the
#   same key. The repository-delete purge spared any key another repository's
#   `artifacts` row also named, without checking that the other repository
#   resolves the key to the same object. On filesystem it does not: each
#   repository has its own file. Repo A's file was spared, the cascade dropped
#   the only row naming it, and GC (which starts from `artifacts` rows) could
#   never find it. Same family on the GC side: the orphan predicate's live-row
#   arm looked across every repository, so a trashed filesystem copy was kept
#   while another repository served its own copy of the key.
#
# Gates:
#   (FIX) after DELETE of repo A, no file is left under A's storage path, and
#   a storage-GC pass does not need to find any. A trashed artifact in repo C
#   whose bytes repo D also holds is reclaimed from C's tree by storage GC.
#   (CONTROL, both images) A's unique file is purged; B still downloads the
#   shared payload byte-exact and its file is on disk; D still downloads its
#   copy; deleting B empties B's tree too (last holder).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: back-to-back admin API calls.
#
# Red: ak-backend:1.11.3. Green: ak-backend:fix-storage.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
